Azure Monitor does NOT automatically collect every log from every Azure resource by default.

Collection depends on:
diagnostic settings
agent configuration
enabled integrations
retention settings
workspace configuration

Azure Monitoring Architecture:
Azure Resource
↓
Diagnostic Settings / Agent
↓
Log Analytics Workspace
↓
Azure Monitor / Sentinel / Alerts / Dashboards

VERY IMPORTANT DIFFERENCE
1. Azure Activity Logs

Automatically available.

Tracks:
resource creation
deletion
RBAC changes
deployments

Example:
Who deleted VM?
Who modified NSG?

2. Resource Logs (Diagnostic Logs)

NOT always enabled by default.

Need:

Diagnostic Settings

to send logs to:
Log Analytics
Storage Account
Event Hub
Example

AKS logs:
kube-audit
kube-apiserver
container insights

Need explicit configuration.


3. VM Logs
Need:
Azure Monitor Agent (AMA)
OR
legacy Log Analytics agent
Without agent:
No OS-level logs.

Example
Linux:
/var/log/messages
syslog

Windows:
Event Viewer logs
Require agent collection.

Enable VM Monitoring Using Azure Monitor + Diagnostic Settings
There are 2 important things people confuse:
| Feature                   | Purpose                             |
| ------------------------- | ----------------------------------- |
| Azure Activity Logs       | Azure resource operations           |
| VM Monitoring/Diagnostics | OS/application-level logs & metrics |

METHOD 1 — Enable VM Monitoring From Portal (Most Common)
Step 1 — Create Log Analytics Workspace
Go:
Azure Portal
→ Log Analytics Workspace
→ Create

Choose:
subscription
resource group
region

Step 2 — Open VM

Go:

Virtual Machine
→ Monitoring
→ Insights

Click: Enable
Azure automatically:
installs Azure Monitor Agent
links Log Analytics Workspace
enables monitoring

Step 3 — Configure Data Collection Rule (DCR)

Choose what to collect:
Linux:
syslog
CPU
memory
disk

Windows:
Event logs
performance counters

Step 4 — Verify Agent
Inside VM:

Linux:
systemctl status azuremonitoragent

Windows:
Check:
Azure Monitor Agent service

Step 5 — Verify Logs

Go:

Azure Monitor
→ Logs

Example query:

For heartbeat:
Heartbeat
| take 10

For proper VM monitoring you usually configure:

Azure Monitor Agent (AMA)
Data Collection Rule (DCR)
Diagnostic Settings (resource logs)
Log Analytics Workspace

| Feature                       | Azure Monitor Agent (AMA)      | Diagnostic Settings                  |
| ----------------------------- | ------------------------------ | ------------------------------------ |
| Purpose                       | Collect guest OS/app telemetry | Collect Azure resource/platform logs |
| Installed where?              | Inside VM/AKS node             | Azure resource configuration         |
| Collects OS logs?             | YES                            | NO                                   |
| Collects app logs?            | YES                            | Limited/resource specific            |
| Collects Azure activity logs? | NO                             | YES                                  |
| Needs agent?                  | YES                            | NO                                   |
| Sends to                      | Log Analytics via DCR          | Log Analytics/EventHub/Storage       |


Interview Scenario
Question

VM logs not appearing in Azure Monitor. How do you troubleshoot?

Strong Answer
Step 1 — Check AMA Agent

Linux:

systemctl status azuremonitoragent

Step 2 — Verify DCR Association

Check:
Data Collection Rule linked?
correct workspace?

Step 3 — Verify Network Connectivity

VM should reach:
Azure Monitor endpoints

Step 4 — Verify Workspace

Run:

Heartbeat

If no heartbeat:
ingestion issue
agent issue

